WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.520
Cells carry cargo, pull chromosomes apart and shorten muscles, and the workers are

2
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:14.140
motor proteins. A motor is an enzyme that turns ATP into steps along a track.

3
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.300
The tracks are actin filaments and microtubules, and each track has a direction.

4
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.580
Myosins walk on actin.

5
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.900
Kinesins and dyneins walk on microtubules.

6
00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.100
So which motor goes which way, and how do we know?

7
00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:32.110
Most myosins move toward the barbed end of actin.

8
00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:37.470
Kinesin-1 moves toward the plus end of a microtubule, so it carries cargo outward.

9
00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:42.570
Cytoplasmic dynein moves toward the minus end, so it carries cargo inward.

10
00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.670
But the family does not always give the direction.

11
00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:50.190
Myosin VI, and a fly protein called ncd, are exceptions.

12
00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:53.310
Direction has to be measured for each motor.

13
00:00:54.333 --> 00:00:55.793
How does one motor move?

14
00:00:56.813 --> 00:00:59.893
Kinesin-1 has two heads that alternate, hand over hand.

15
00:01:00.793 --> 00:01:04.413
One head holds on while the other moves, so the motor is processive.

16
00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:06.993
It takes many steps without letting go.

17
00:01:07.793 --> 00:01:13.313
The cargo advances 8 nanometres per step, and each step costs one ATP at low load.

18
00:01:14.193 --> 00:01:16.913
These numbers come from purified proteins, in vitro.

19
00:01:18.017 --> 00:01:20.937
To watch one motor step, use an optical trap.

20
00:01:21.777 --> 00:01:26.977
A focused laser holds a small bead, and a bead pulled off centre feels a force, like

21
00:01:26.977 --> 00:01:27.977
a weight on a spring.

22
00:01:28.717 --> 00:01:30.757
The force is the stiffness times the distance.

23
00:01:31.677 --> 00:01:36.857
In a trap, kinesin beads advanced in 8-nanometre steps, and the motor kept going

24
00:01:36.857 --> 00:01:39.477
against loads up to 5 to 6 piconewtons.

25
00:01:40.317 --> 00:01:46.677
With made-up numbers, a stiffness of 0.05 piconewtons per nanometre and a stall at

26
00:01:46.677 --> 00:01:49.537
120 nanometres give 6 piconewtons.

27
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.210
Motors rarely work alone.

28
00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:58.190
In reconstitution experiments, purified human dynein becomes a fast, processive

29
00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:03.530
motor when it binds dynactin and an activating adaptor, a protein that also binds

30
00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:08.050
cargo. Some adaptors recruit two dyneins, and that gave more force.

31
00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:12.390
In muscle, millions of myosin heads each pull once and let go.

32
00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:17.110
The thin filaments slide past the thick ones, so no filament gets shorter.

33
00:02:18.183 --> 00:02:19.363
Keep three things.

34
00:02:20.523 --> 00:02:23.023
Motors turn ATP into steps along polar tracks.

35
00:02:23.723 --> 00:02:27.483
The family gives the track, but the direction is measured for each motor.

36
00:02:28.383 --> 00:02:33.143
And every number belongs to the preparation that measured it: one motor, in vitro,

37
00:02:33.763 --> 00:02:35.623
under the loading condition that was measured.
